1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,206
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:04,206 --> 00:00:08,963
This is chapter thirty-three: scoped values, final in Java 25.

3
00:00:09,413 --> 00:00:18,894
Every server needs to carry context, like a trace ID, a tenant, or the logged-in user, through layers that shouldn't take it as a parameter.

4
00:00:18,894 --> 00:00:21,873
For twenty years the answer was ThreadLocal.

5
00:00:21,873 --> 00:00:26,885
Virtual threads broke its assumptions, and ScopedValue is the replacement.

6
00:00:27,785 --> 00:00:30,410
Here's the classic ThreadLocal pattern.

7
00:00:30,410 --> 00:00:35,593
Set the request context, handle the request, and clear it in a finally block.

8
00:00:36,043 --> 00:00:38,386
It has four compounding problems.

9
00:00:38,386 --> 00:00:40,232
Its lifetime is unbounded.

10
00:00:40,232 --> 00:00:45,772
It stays set until someone removes it, and any refactor can drop that finally.

11
00:00:45,772 --> 00:00:52,660
And on a pooled thread, if remove is missed, the next request sees the previous tenant's context.

12
00:00:52,660 --> 00:00:56,779
That's a correctness bug that is also a security incident.

13
00:00:57,229 --> 00:01:03,857
Anyone can write to it, so context can be changed three layers down without the caller knowing.

14
00:01:03,857 --> 00:01:08,881
And InheritableThreadLocal copies the whole map into every child thread.

15
00:01:08,881 --> 00:01:12,439
Invisible at two hundred threads, not at a million.

16
00:01:13,339 --> 00:01:16,887
A ScopedValue is bound for the duration of a block.

17
00:01:16,887 --> 00:01:21,408
Create a static final key, then call where with a value, and run.

18
00:01:21,408 --> 00:01:27,599
Everything that block calls can read it, and when the block returns, the binding is gone.

19
00:01:27,599 --> 00:01:29,617
There's no set and no remove.

20
00:01:30,067 --> 00:01:35,538
Compared to ThreadLocal: it's bound once per scope instead of mutable anywhere.

21
00:01:35,538 --> 00:01:38,031
Its lifetime is the enclosing block.

22
00:01:38,031 --> 00:01:40,871
Cleanup is automatic, even on exceptions.

23
00:01:40,871 --> 00:01:46,342
And because the value can't change, child tasks share it instead of copying it.

24
00:01:46,342 --> 00:01:49,736
That's what makes it viable at a million threads.

25
00:01:50,636 --> 00:01:53,181
Here's request context in practice.

26
00:01:53,181 --> 00:01:57,325
The handler binds the context and runs the order service.

27
00:01:57,325 --> 00:02:00,960
Four layers down, the repository reads the tenant.

28
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:06,849
No finally, no remove, and nothing in between carries a parameter it doesn't use.

29
00:02:07,299 --> 00:02:09,647
That's essentially the whole API.

30
00:02:09,647 --> 00:02:11,355
where, then run or call.

31
00:02:11,355 --> 00:02:16,192
get reads the value, and throws if it's unbound, so it's never null.

32
00:02:16,192 --> 00:02:20,603
And isBound, orElse and orElseThrow for testing and fallbacks.

33
00:02:20,603 --> 00:02:22,595
There's deliberately no set.

34
00:02:23,495 --> 00:02:26,189
Rebinding shadows instead of mutating.

35
00:02:26,189 --> 00:02:33,207
A nightly job binds a system context, then for each tenant binds a narrower one for that iteration.

36
00:02:33,207 --> 00:02:37,034
When the inner run returns, the outer binding is back.

37
00:02:37,034 --> 00:02:41,287
With ThreadLocal that's save, set, and restore in a finally.

38
00:02:41,737 --> 00:02:45,085
And it's designed to fit structured concurrency.

39
00:02:45,085 --> 00:02:49,340
Every subtask forked inside the scope reads the same binding.

40
00:02:49,340 --> 00:02:55,199
Nothing is copied, because the value is immutable, so all three share one reference.

41
00:02:56,099 --> 00:03:00,979
Migration is mechanical where a ThreadLocal is set once per request.

42
00:03:00,979 --> 00:03:04,568
Set, try, finally, remove becomes where, then run.

43
00:03:05,018 --> 00:03:09,242
get returning null becomes get throwing, or orElse with a default.

44
00:03:09,692 --> 00:03:11,911
Checking for null becomes isBound.

45
00:03:12,361 --> 00:03:17,502
And InheritableThreadLocal becomes automatic inheritance into subtasks.

46
00:03:17,952 --> 00:03:24,759
What doesn't map is anything written more than once during a request, like a reusable per-thread buffer.

47
00:03:24,759 --> 00:03:29,864
That's mutable per-thread state, which ScopedValue deliberately can't express.

48
00:03:29,864 --> 00:03:34,315
And on virtual threads, a buffer per thread needs rethinking anyway.

49
00:03:35,215 --> 00:03:37,733
Why does one leak and the other can't?

50
00:03:37,733 --> 00:03:42,240
Each thread holds a ThreadLocalMap with weak keys and strong values.

51
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:50,592
When the ThreadLocal itself becomes unreachable, the key is collected but the value lingers until the map happens to clean up.

52
00:03:50,592 --> 00:03:53,309
On an idle pooled thread, that's forever.

53
00:03:53,309 --> 00:03:58,148
That's how one ThreadLocal pins a whole classloader after a hot redeploy.

54
00:03:58,598 --> 00:04:06,738
A scoped binding is a small immutable node pushed when run is entered and popped when it exits, tied to that stack frame.

55
00:04:06,738 --> 00:04:10,708
It can't outlive the frame, so there's nothing to clean up.

56
00:04:10,708 --> 00:04:14,811
And reads are cached, approaching the cost of a field access.

57
00:04:15,261 --> 00:04:16,960
It's final in Java 25.

58
00:04:16,960 --> 00:04:24,371
Samples using callWhere, or a three-argument where, are from earlier previews and won't compile.

59
00:04:25,271 --> 00:04:26,579
Production rules.

60
00:04:26,579 --> 00:04:30,196
Use ScopedValue for request context on Java 25.

61
00:04:30,196 --> 00:04:34,967
Keep keys static final, and values immutable, ideally records.

62
00:04:34,967 --> 00:04:37,814
Use orElseThrow with a clear message.

63
00:04:37,814 --> 00:04:41,585
Rebind to narrow the context, never to mutate it.

64
00:04:41,585 --> 00:04:44,663
And pair it with structured concurrency.

65
00:04:45,113 --> 00:04:49,569
Never use ThreadLocal for context without remove in a finally.

66
00:04:49,569 --> 00:04:53,306
Never bind mutable objects, which defeats the point.

67
00:04:53,306 --> 00:04:56,900
Avoid InheritableThreadLocal with virtual threads.

68
00:04:56,900 --> 00:05:00,494
And never keep per-thread caches of large objects.

69
00:05:01,394 --> 00:05:02,033
To recap.

70
00:05:02,033 --> 00:05:07,782
A scoped value is bound for a block, then gone, so there's no remove and no leak.

71
00:05:07,782 --> 00:05:12,182
It's immutable, so callees can't change what the caller bound.

72
00:05:12,182 --> 00:05:15,234
Subtasks share the binding without copying.

73
00:05:15,234 --> 00:05:20,061
And ThreadLocal remains only for genuinely mutable per-thread state.

74
00:05:20,511 --> 00:05:23,019
That completes the concurrency section.

75
00:05:23,019 --> 00:05:28,227
Next, the runtime section, starting with chapter thirty-four: garbage collection.

76
00:05:28,227 --> 00:05:29,513
Thanks for watching.
